from scriptkit import sc, ch, save, S

# Grandma Ruth 87 · ANGLE: disguise story (owner goes undercover as a cleaning lady in her own company to choose a successor)

sc("""Sweetheart, my cousin Marjorie owned thirty-one grocery stores across Ohio. And at seventy-six years old, she put on a gray uniform and a name tag that said Margie, and took a job cleaning the floors at her own headquarters, at night, for eleven dollars an hour. Not one person in that building knew who she was. She was looking for the person who would run her company after she was gone.""",
   "elderly cleaning lady mopping office floor at night", "SHE CLEANED HER OWN FLOORS")
sc("""Tonight I'm going to tell you what Marjorie saw with a mop in her hand, the vice president who made her clean up his coffee twice, and the young woman from the mail room who did one small thing that changed her life. Stay with me, honey, because the board meeting at the end of this story is the most satisfying thing I've ever witnessed.""",
   "corporate boardroom long table", "THE BOARD MEETING")

ch("Marjorie")
sc("""Marjorie and I grew up together in Mansfield, sweetheart. Our mothers were sisters. In nineteen sixty-two, she and her husband Frank opened one little grocery store on the corner of Fourth and Main, with a cash register Frank bought used and a sign Marjorie painted herself. They worked sixteen hours a day. Marjorie knew every customer's name and every child's birthday.""",
   "1960s corner grocery store hand painted sign", "ONE LITTLE STORE")
sc("""By the time Frank died, there were thirty-one stores, two thousand employees, and a big glass headquarters building in Columbus. And Marjorie had a problem. She had no children. And she had three vice presidents, all in fine suits, all very polite to her, all telling her they were the right person to run the company. In a minute I'll tell you which one she suspected.""",
   "glass office building headquarters", "THREE VICE PRESIDENTS")
sc("""She said to me, Ruth, every one of them smiles at me in the boardroom. But Frank always said, you don't know a man until you've seen how he treats the people who can't do anything for him. And in that building, I'm the one everybody wants something from. I'll never see the truth from the top floor. I have to see it from the floor.""",
   "two elderly women talking over coffee", "SEE IT FROM THE FLOOR")

ch("Margie")
sc("""So she did it, honey. She told the board she was going to Florida for three months. She dyed her white hair brown, put on glasses she didn't need, and applied for a night cleaning job through an agency, under her mother's maiden name. Margie Holt. Nobody at headquarters had seen her in person more than a few times. She walked right in and got a mop.""",
   "elderly woman putting on glasses and uniform", "MARGIE HOLT")
sc("""She worked from six at night until two in the morning. Emptying trash cans. Vacuuming offices. Cleaning bathrooms. And she said the strangest thing happened the very first night. She became invisible. People walked past her like she was a piece of furniture. They talked on their phones about everything, right in front of her, as if she couldn't hear.""",
   "office workers ignoring cleaning lady", "SHE BECAME INVISIBLE")

sc("""And she learned things, honey. Things no report ever told her. That the heat in the warehouse office had been broken for two winters. That the night security guard was working two jobs because his wife needed dialysis. That the coffee in the break room was paid for by the cleaning crew out of their own pockets. She wrote it all down in a little notebook in her uniform pocket.""",
   "small notebook in uniform pocket", "THINGS NO REPORT TOLD HER")
ch("The vice presidents")
sc("""If you're enjoying Grandma's story, tap the like button for me, sweetheart. It helps more people find these stories. The first vice president was a man named Grant. Tall, handsome, always smiling in meetings. One night, Marjorie was mopping outside his office, and he walked out with a full cup of coffee, bumped into her mop bucket, and spilled it all over the floor she'd just cleaned.""",
   "coffee spilled on clean office floor", "HE SPILLED IT")
sc("""He didn't say sorry, honey. He looked down at it, then at her, and he said, well, don't just stand there. Then he got a second cup, and on his way back, he dropped the empty cup right on the floor next to her bucket, and walked over it. On purpose. She heard him on the phone a minute later, saying, the old lady's in Florida. Once the board votes, I'm cutting the night staff to save money.""",
   "man in suit dropping cup on floor", "DON'T JUST STAND THERE")
sc("""The second vice president was a woman named Lauren. She never spilled anything. She was polite. But in three months, she never once said good evening to Margie, never once looked at her face. And one night, Marjorie heard her tell another manager to fire a cashier at the Dayton store, a single mother who'd been late four times because her baby was sick. Lauren said, rules are rules. Sentiment is expensive.""",
   "woman executive on phone cold expression", "SENTIMENT IS EXPENSIVE")
sc("""The third vice president, Doug, was friendly enough. He'd say, evening, ma'am. But one Friday night, Marjorie overheard him on the phone with a supplier, and she realized he'd been taking money on the side to put their products on the best shelves. Twenty years with the company, honey. And he'd been stealing from her and Frank the whole time.""",
   "man whispering on phone in dark office", "TAKING MONEY ON THE SIDE")

ch("Rosa")
sc("""So far, three vice presidents, and not one of them she'd trust with a single store. Marjorie came to my house that Sunday and cried at my kitchen table. She said, Ruth, Frank and I built that place with our hands. And the people at the top are the worst ones in the building. And then she said, but there's a girl in the mail room I can't stop thinking about.""",
   "elderly woman crying at kitchen table", "THE MAIL ROOM GIRL")
sc("""Her name was Rosa. She was twenty-six, she sorted mail on the first floor, and she was taking night classes in business at the community college. She studied at the little table by the vending machines on her dinner break, at eight o'clock every night, right where Marjorie took her break too. And the very first night, Rosa looked up from her textbook and said, hi. I'm Rosa. What's your name?""",
   "young woman studying textbook by vending machines", "WHAT'S YOUR NAME?")
sc("""Every night after that, Rosa saved her a seat. She asked about Margie's family. She shared her dinner, rice and beans from home, because Margie only had crackers. She told Margie about her mother, who cleaned houses in Columbus and had raised four kids alone. And she said, my mom always says, the people who clean the building know more about it than the people who own it.""",
   "two women sharing dinner at break table", "SHE SAVED HER A SEAT")

ch("The small thing")
sc("""And then, the small thing I promised you. One night in December, it was snowing hard, and the buses had stopped. Margie was standing at the door at two in the morning, with no way home. Rosa had stayed late to study. She saw her standing there in her thin coat, and she said, Margie, I'll drive you home. Where do you live? And Marjorie panicked, because she lived in a mansion.""",
   "young woman offering ride in snowy parking lot night", "I'LL DRIVE YOU HOME")
sc("""So Marjorie gave her my address, honey. In Mansfield. An hour away in the snow. Rosa didn't blink. She drove an old Honda with a heater that barely worked, an hour each way, at two in the morning, in a snowstorm. And when she dropped Margie off at my door, she pressed something into her hand. It was twenty dollars. She said, for groceries. I know cleaning doesn't pay much.""",
   "old car dropping woman off at small house snow", "FOR GROCERIES")
sc("""Twenty dollars, sweetheart. From a girl making fourteen dollars an hour, with student loans, sending money home to her mother. Marjorie came into my kitchen at three in the morning, still holding that twenty-dollar bill, and she sat down and didn't say anything for a long time. And then she said, Ruth, I found her. And I said, found who? And she said, the one Frank would have picked.""",
   "elderly woman holding twenty dollar bill kitchen night", "I FOUND HER")

sc("""For the rest of the winter, Marjorie watched Rosa closely, sweetheart. She saw her help a lost delivery driver find the loading dock. She saw her stay late to fix a mistake somebody else had made in the mail. She saw her give her umbrella to the security guard. And not once, in three months, did Rosa ever do a kind thing when anybody important was watching. There was nobody important there. Just Margie.""",
   "young woman giving umbrella to security guard", "NOBODY WAS WATCHING")
ch("The board meeting")
sc("""In March, Marjorie came back from Florida, with her white hair and her pearls and her good suit, and called a meeting of the board and all three vice presidents. I was there, sitting in the back, because she asked me to come. Grant was smiling. Lauren had a folder. Doug was sweating a little. Each of them thought they were about to be named the next president.""",
   "boardroom executives waiting for meeting", "EACH THOUGHT IT WAS THEM")
sc("""Marjorie stood up at the head of that long table and said, I wasn't in Florida. For three months, I cleaned this building at night. And she put a gray uniform on the table, with a name tag that said Margie. Grant's face went the color of paste. Lauren dropped her pen. And Doug, honey, Doug looked at the door like he was planning to run through it.""",
   "cleaning uniform with name tag on boardroom table", "I WASN'T IN FLORIDA")
sc("""She looked at Grant and said, you spilled coffee on my clean floor and told me not to just stand there. And then you threw your cup at my feet. Grant, you're done. She looked at Lauren and said, the cashier in Dayton gets her job back, with back pay. And you can find a company where sentiment isn't so expensive. And then she handed Doug an envelope from the company lawyers, and said, I think you know what this is.""",
   "executives shocked at boardroom table", "YOU'RE DONE")

ch("Rosa's surprise")
sc("""Then Marjorie pressed a button on the phone and said, send her up. And the boardroom door opened, and in came Rosa from the mail room, in her best blouse, scared to death, because nobody from the mail room had ever been called to the top floor. She looked around that room, and she saw Marjorie at the head of the table in pearls, and she said, Margie?""",
   "young woman entering boardroom nervously", "MARGIE?")
sc("""Marjorie walked around that table and gave Rosa back her twenty-dollar bill. Then she told the board that the company would pay for the rest of Rosa's college, and her master's degree after that. And that Rosa would spend the next five years working beside her, learning every store, every job, from the loading dock to the boardroom. And then she'd be president.""",
   "older woman handing young woman money boardroom", "THEN SHE'D BE PRESIDENT")

ch("Grandma's lesson")
sc("""Rosa has run that company for six years now, sweetheart. Thirty-eight stores. She raised the night cleaning staff's pay the first week. And every new manager, before they start, has to spend one full night cleaning the headquarters with a mop. Rosa calls it the Margie shift. Marjorie passed in twenty twenty-two, and Rosa gave the eulogy. She held up a twenty-dollar bill.""",
   "young woman CEO speaking at podium", "THE MARGIE SHIFT")
sc("""Here's what I want you to remember, honey. Everybody's kind to the boss. What matters is how you treat the person holding the mop. Because one day, you might find out who's holding it. Tell me in the comments, sweetheart, how do the people at your work treat the cleaning staff? There are more of my family's stories in the playlist right there on your screen, and I'll see you in the next one.""",
   "mop and bucket in empty office hallway", "WHO HOLDS THE MOP?")

